<h2>Why hail documentation matters on the Coastal Bend</h2>

<p>Hail in South Texas is often very localized. One subdivision off Saratoga can take golf ball hail while a neighborhood a few miles away sees only rain. The adjuster reviewing your file was not there, and the storm may not have produced a report on your block. Your evidence has to stand on its own.</p>

<p>Timing is also seasonal. According to the NOAA NCEI Storm Events Database, Nueces County has logged 134 hail reports since the 1950s, and 86 of them came in April or May (May 58, April 28). San Patricio and Kleberg counties show the same spring peak. Those are the months to keep a camera ready. Hail does fall outside spring, though: on November 1, 2025, the Mustang Island State Park emergency manager estimated hail around 3 inches near the <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=1297578" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Port Aransas airport</a>, the largest hail size in the Nueces County record.</p>

<p>Strong documentation does four things:</p>
<ul>
<li>Shows that damage exists, in a way a reviewer can verify.</li>
<li>Connects that damage to a specific storm date.</li>
<li>Shows how far the damage extends across the roof, walls, and contents.</li>
<li>Supports the repair or replacement cost in the estimate.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Know who you are documenting for: TWIA or your homeowners carrier</h2>

<p>On the Texas coast, the first question is not "how many photos?" but "which company covers hail on this property?" The Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) covers wind and hail losses only, for eligible properties in the designated catastrophe area, which includes Nueces, San Patricio, Aransas, and Kleberg counties. Many coastal homeowners carry a homeowners policy that excludes wind and hail, a separate TWIA policy, and a flood policy. A hail claim on that kind of home goes to TWIA. Other owners have a private policy that includes wind and hail. Check your declarations page before you file.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Who covers hail</th><th>Key timing rules</th><th>What it means for documentation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>TWIA windstorm policy</td><td>File no later than one year after the damage occurs; TWIA has 60 days to accept or deny after receiving the claim or requested information (Tex. Ins. Code §2210.573)</td><td>Your file needs to show the date of loss clearly. A late-discovered hail claim needs strong evidence tying damage to a specific storm.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Private homeowners or commercial policy with wind/hail</td><td>Insurer must acknowledge within 15 days and generally decide within 15 business days after receiving all items it requested (Tex. Ins. Code §542.055–.056)</td><td>Respond to every information request in writing and keep copies, because the clock runs from receipt of requested items.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>You can check your own timeline with our <a href="/claim-deadline-calculator/">claim deadline calculator</a>, and our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page explains the TWIA process in more detail.</p>

<h2>Safety first</h2>

<p>No photo is worth a fall. Wet or algae-covered shingles are slick, metal roofs are slicker, and summer heat on a dark roof is punishing. Stay off the roof if you are not trained and equipped. Watch for downed power lines and hanging limbs after a storm. You can gather a lot of strong evidence from the ground, from a second-story window, and inside the attic with a flashlight.</p>

<h2>What hail damage looks like on Coastal Bend homes</h2>

<p>Before you start shooting photos, know what you are looking for. Not every mark is hail, and real hail damage is often subtle. Insurers will also look for signs of age, salt-air corrosion, foot traffic, or manufacturing defects, so it helps to document what is new and what is not.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>What hail damage can look like</th><th>How to photograph it</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Composition (asphalt) shingles</td><td>Dark spots where granules were knocked loose, soft "bruised" areas, fractured mats, cracked edges</td><td>Wide shot of the slope, then a close-up with a coin or chalk circle for scale</td></tr>
<tr><td>Metal roofing and flashing</td><td>Round dents, chipped coating, damaged seams</td><td>Low-angle light makes dents visible; shoot across the panel, not straight down</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gutters, downspouts, roof vents</td><td>Dents on the top and storm-facing sides</td><td>Close-ups with a ruler; these help show hail size and direction</td></tr>
<tr><td>AC condensers</td><td>Flattened or bent fins on top and sides</td><td>Photograph the whole unit, then a close-up of the fins</td></tr>
<tr><td>Siding, stucco, fiber cement, window screens</td><td>Cracks, chips, spatter marks, torn or dented screens</td><td>Document each elevation with the direction (north, east, etc.) noted</td></tr>
<tr><td>Interior and attic</td><td>New ceiling stains, wet insulation, daylight through decking, drips at penetrations</td><td>Photos with a date reference, plus video of active leaks</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

