<h2>Step-by-step: how to document hail damage for an insurance claim</h2>

<p>Follow these steps in order and you will end up with a file that an adjuster, a roofer, and, if needed, an appraiser can all work from.</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Record the storm.</strong> Write down the date, approximate start and end time, hail size, wind direction, and how long the hail lasted. If you can do so safely, photograph hailstones next to a ruler or coin before they melt.</li>
<li><strong>Take wide shots.</strong> Photograph each side of the building from the street or yard, plus the roof slopes you can see from the ground. These establish context.</li>
<li><strong>Cover every elevation.</strong> Front, rear, left, and right. Hail is usually driven by wind, so one or two sides may take most of the impacts. Skipping a side can mean missing legitimate damage.</li>
<li><strong>Move in for medium and close-up shots.</strong> For each damaged area, take a mid-range photo that shows where it is, then a close-up with a coin, ruler, or tape measure for scale.</li>
<li><strong>Document soft metals.</strong> Gutters, downspouts, vent caps, the AC condenser, mailboxes, and metal fences are often the clearest proof of hail size and direction.</li>
<li><strong>Check inside.</strong> Look at ceilings, walls near windows, and the attic. Photograph stains, wet insulation, and any light coming through the decking.</li>
<li><strong>Shoot a walkaround video.</strong> Narrate the date, the address, and what you are seeing as you walk the property. Video helps an adjuster understand how the photos fit together.</li>
<li><strong>Protect the property and keep receipts.</strong> Tarp leaks or board broken windows if needed. Photograph the damage first, then the temporary repair, and keep every receipt.</li>
</ol>

<p>Keep your phone's location and timestamp settings on, and do not edit, filter, or crop the original files. If you need marked-up versions for a presentation, save copies and keep the originals untouched.</p>

