<h2>Tie the damage to a date with NOAA storm records</h2>

<p>An insurer may ask whether the damage came from the storm you reported, an older storm, or simple wear. Weather records help answer that. The NOAA NCEI Storm Events Database lists hail reports by date, location, and size. A few Nueces County examples show how specific these records can be:</p>

<ul>
<li>On April 24, 2016, a spotter photographed hail up to baseball size (2.75 inches) <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=632317" target="_blank" rel="noopener">west of Calallen near County Road 75 and FM 624</a>.</li>
<li>On June 4, 2017, the public reported golf ball hail <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=713211" target="_blank" rel="noopener">near Alameda Street and Airline Road</a> in Corpus Christi.</li>
<li>On April 22, 2015, golf ball hail fell <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=573942" target="_blank" rel="noopener">on Chapman Ranch Road before reaching Staples Street</a>.</li>
</ul>

<p>Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> can help you find reports near your address for your date of loss. Print or save the report and add it to your file. Remember that these are point reports. No report on your street does not mean no hail fell there, which is why your own photos of soft-metal dents matter.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Document-Hail-Damage-for-an-Insurance-Claim-4-1024x683.webp" alt="Collage of hail claim documentation: chalk-circled shingle impacts, dented gutters and siding, AC fins, attic insulation, and a notebook with a tape measure" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A complete hail file covers the roof, soft metals, siding, the AC unit, screens, the attic, and written notes, not just a few shingle photos.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Organize your claim file</h2>

<p>A pile of 300 unlabeled photos is hard for anyone to review. Organize your evidence into folders so an adjuster can follow it quickly:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>01 Storm:</strong> your notes, hailstone photos, NOAA reports, and any local news or NWS statements for the date.</li>
<li><strong>02 Roof:</strong> one subfolder per slope, labeled by direction (north slope, south slope, and so on).</li>
<li><strong>03 Exterior:</strong> gutters, siding, windows, screens, fences, AC unit, by elevation.</li>
<li><strong>04 Interior:</strong> each room and the attic.</li>
<li><strong>05 Mitigation:</strong> tarp and board-up photos, receipts, and invoices.</li>
<li><strong>06 Insurance:</strong> the policy and declarations page, claim number, letters, emails, the adjuster's estimate, and a contact log with dates and names.</li>
</ul>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> gives you a printable version of this list. Back everything up to cloud storage, since phones get lost and hard drives fail.</p>

<h2>Common documentation mistakes</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Waiting weeks to look.</strong> Later storms and normal wear make it harder to tie damage to one date.</li>
<li><strong>Only photographing the roof.</strong> Soft metals and siding often tell the clearest story.</li>
<li><strong>Close-ups without context.</strong> A photo of a single bruise with no wider shot cannot be placed on the roof.</li>
<li><strong>No scale reference.</strong> Without a coin or ruler, the size of an impact is guesswork.</li>
<li><strong>Permanent repairs before inspection.</strong> Replacing the roof before the insurer inspects can make the claim much harder to prove.</li>
<li><strong>Sending evidence to the wrong insurer.</strong> If TWIA covers hail on your property, a claim filed only with your homeowners carrier may not start the right clock.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster can help</h2>

<p>Many hail claims are handled directly between the owner and the insurer. Outside help makes more sense when the damage is extensive, the insurer's estimate looks thin, the claim was denied, or a commercial property is involved. A licensed public adjuster represents the policyholder, not the insurance company, and can inspect the property, document the loss with tools like drone photos and Xactimate estimates, review the policy, and present the claim. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

